<section class="hero"><div class="eyebrow">ANTICIPATE ORIGINAL RESEARCH / THE MODEL ROOM</div>
<h1>Tyrese Haliburton: A Career-Ending Injury? Or a <em>Career-Defining One?</em></h1>
<p class="dek">A torn Achilles can change everything. But how much does it really change the player who comes back?</p>
<div class="meta"><span>NBA ANALYTICS</span><span>2026–27 SEASON</span><span>24 HISTORICAL CASES</span><span>OCTOBER 2026</span></div>
<figure class="hero-image"><img src="https://images.assettype.com/tribune/2025-06-23/dosvozbv/0-02-06-0c75b444bcd99a2496a22a001bbb3212dd96418968c09ed0ad3ec66215837d6c2724db3388f0a04c.jpg?w=1200&amp;auto=format" alt="Tyrese Haliburton at the 2025 NBA Finals" fetchpriority="high"/>
<figcaption class="caption">Tyrese Haliburton during the 2025 NBA Finals. Image hosted by <a href="https://www.tribuneindia.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Tribune</a> / Assettype. Photographer and reuse rights are not established by this credit; confirm permission before public commercial publication.</figcaption></figure>
</section>
<section class="content"><article class="prose">
<p class="opening">When Tyrese Haliburton went down in Game 7 of the Finals, it was hard not to wonder if we'd ever see the same player again.</p>
<p>A torn Achilles has that kind of reputation in basketball. It's an injury that can change a career, and even when a player makes it back, nobody really knows how much of their old game they'll have left.</p>
<p>Haliburton tore his right Achilles in Game 7 of the 2025 NBA Finals and missed the following season. What stuck with me was how quickly the conversation changed. One minute people were talking about the Pacers' Finals run, and the next they were wondering what kind of player Haliburton would be when he returned.</p>
<p>I've always found his game interesting because he doesn't need to be the fastest or strongest guy on the floor. He reads defenses, finds his teammates and seems to know where the ball should go before anyone else does. I wondered whether that could make his return a little different, so I started looking at what happened to other players after the same injury.</p>
<h2>Before the injury</h2>
<p>Haliburton averaged 18.6 points and 9.2 assists in the 2024–25 regular season, shooting 38.8% from three. What stands out to me is how much he does for the players around him. He can have a great game without needing to take every shot.</p>
<div class="stats"><div class="stat"><strong>18.6</strong><span>POINTS PER GAME<br/>2024–25</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>9.2</strong><span>ASSISTS PER GAME<br/>2024–25</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>38.8%</strong><span>THREE-POINT SHOOTING<br/>2024–25</span></div></div>
<p>I didn't want to compare players only by their points per game, since someone coming back from an injury might be playing fewer minutes. So for this project, I looked at their numbers <strong>per 36 minutes</strong>. It's not a perfect comparison, but it helps.</p>
<h2>What can NBA history tell us?</h2>
<p>The first names people bring up are usually Kevin Durant and Kobe Bryant. Durant returned looking like himself. Kobe's comeback was much harder. Both stories are real, but neither one tells us what will happen to Haliburton. That's why I wanted to look beyond the famous examples.</p>
<p>I ended up with <strong>24 NBA players</strong> in my dataset. I looked at their age, position, size, how long they took to return and what their numbers looked like before and after the injury. Twenty-four players isn't enough to give a definite answer, but I thought it was enough to start noticing some patterns.</p>
<div class="chart"><h3>01 / Before and after the Achilles</h3><small>Historical sample means · production per 36 minutes · n = 24</small>
{statPairs.map(s=><div class="bar-row"><label>{s.label}</label><div class="bar-pair"><div class="bar before" style={`width:${s.before/22*100}%`}>{s.before.toFixed(1)}</div><div class="bar after" style={`width:${s.after/22*100}%`}>{s.after.toFixed(1)}</div></div></div>)}
<div class="legend"><span><i class="swatch" style="background:#85897b"></i>Before injury</span><span><i class="swatch" style="background:#d4ff45"></i>After injury</span></div>
<p class="chart-note">Source: <a href="https://github.com/mudia-OE/Hali-Predict/blob/main/achilles_dataset.csv">Hali Predict historical dataset</a>. These are unweighted averages across players, not season-level league averages.</p></div>
<p>The biggest change was in scoring. Players went from about <strong>19.9 to 17.2 points per 36 minutes</strong> on average. Assists barely changed, staying around 3.9, and rebounds were almost the same too. That doesn't mean passing is unaffected by the injury, but it was interesting to see scoring drop more than the other two stats.</p>
<h2>Looking for patterns in the numbers</h2>
<p>Age was the first thing I checked, but I also wanted to know whether height, weight and position made a difference. In the graphs below, a positive number means a player scored more points per 36 minutes after returning; a negative number means they scored less. These are comparisons within my 24-player sample, not rules that apply to every NBA player.</p>
<div class="chart"><h3>02 / Age and scoring change</h3><small>Age at injury versus change in points per 36 · each dot represents one player</small>
<svg class="scatter" viewBox="0 0 690 300" role="img" aria-label="Scatter plot showing age at injury against change in points per 36 minutes">
{[-10,-5,0,5].map(t=><g><line x1="58" y1={245-(t+11)/16*190} x2="625" y2={245-(t+11)/16*190} stroke={t===0?"#d4ff45":"#45483e"} stroke-dasharray={t===0?"5 5":"2 6"}/><text x="48" y={249-(t+11)/16*190} text-anchor="end" fill="#b8b9af" font-size="12">{t>0?"+":""}{t}</text></g>)}
{[20,25,30,35].map(t=><text x={58+(t-20)/18*565} y="270" text-anchor="middle" fill="#b8b9af" font-size="12">{t}</text>)}
