<line x1="225.4074074074074" x2="225.4074074074074" y1="22" y2="245" stroke="#ffffff" stroke-dasharray="4 5" opacity=".75"/>
<text x="340" y="295" text-anchor="middle" fill="#d4d5ca" font-size="13">Weight (pounds)</text><text x="13" y="145" transform="rotate(-90 13 145)" text-anchor="middle" fill="#d4d5ca" font-size="12">Change in points / 36</text></svg><p class="chart-note">Pearson correlation: <strong>r ≈ −0.15</strong>. Dashed vertical line marks Haliburton's weight. At 185 pounds, Haliburton is lighter than most of the players in the sample. The very weak negative correlation does not establish that lighter players recover better.</p></div>
<div class="chart"><h3>05 / Position and scoring change</h3><small>Mean post-minus-pre points per 36 by position · sample sizes shown</small>
{[{pos:"PG",code:"pg"},{pos:"SG",code:"sg"},{pos:"SF",code:"sf"},{pos:"PF",code:"pf"},{pos:"C",code:"c"}].map(({pos,code})=>{const group=players.filter(p=>p.position===code);const avg=group.reduce((sum,p)=>sum+p.post-p.pre,0)/group.length;return <div class="position-row"><span>{pos} <small>n={group.length}</small></span><div class="position-track"><div class="position-zero"></div><div class="position-bar" style={avg<0?`right:50%;width:${Math.abs(avg)/10*48}%`:`left:50%;width:${avg/10*48}%`}></div></div><strong>{avg>0?"+":""}{avg.toFixed(1)}</strong></div>})}
<p class="chart-note">Point guards (n=5) averaged a <strong>1.9-point decline</strong>, shooting guards (n=5) 2.3, small forwards (n=4) a 0.2-point increase, power forwards (n=4) a 3.1-point decline and centers (n=6) a 5.5-point decline. Haliburton is a <strong>point guard</strong>, so the PG group is the most directly relevant positional comparison, although it is only five players.</p></div>
<p>Of these comparisons, position stood out to me most. Centers had the largest average scoring drop, while point guards had a smaller decline and small forwards were roughly unchanged. But these groups are tiny, and differences between them could reflect the specific players involved rather than the position itself.</p>
<p>For Haliburton, the profile is a <strong>25-year-old, 77-inch, 185-pound point guard</strong>. His age and position put him in groups with smaller average scoring losses than the oldest players and centers in this sample. Height and weight add context, but neither shows a strong linear relationship with scoring change. That's why Hali Predict considers these characteristics together with recovery time and his pre-injury production, instead of trying to predict his comeback from any one chart.</p>
<h2>Building Hali Predict</h2>
<p>I wanted to try making an actual prediction instead of just comparing players, so I built <a href="https://github.com/mudia-OE/Hali-Predict">Hali Predict</a>. It's a Python model that looks at how players' numbers changed after an Achilles tear. I used <strong>ridge regression</strong> because I was working with a small dataset and didn't want to make the model more complicated than it needed to be.</p>
<p>I gave it each player's age, height, weight, position, recovery time and pre-injury stats. It then tried to predict <strong>how much their numbers would change</strong>, rather than treating every player like they started at the same level.</p>
<p>To see how well it worked, I tested it on each player one at a time, training it on the other 23 first. This is called <strong>leave-one-out cross-validation</strong>. I wanted to see how close it could get without already knowing the answer.</p>
<div class="method"><strong>MODEL VALIDATION / MEAN ABSOLUTE ERROR</strong><p>Points: <strong>2.70</strong> per 36 · Assists: <strong>0.94</strong> per 36 · Rebounds: <strong>1.37</strong> per 36. These are average absolute errors on held-out historical cases, not guarantees for Haliburton.</p></div>
<h2>What does the model predict?</h2>
<p>In his last healthy season, Haliburton averaged <strong>21.6 points, 11.1 assists and 4.0 rebounds per 36 minutes</strong>. That's the baseline I want to compare his comeback against.</p>
<p>Hali Predict's model estimates <strong>23.4 points, 10.1 assists and 4.4 rebounds per 36 minutes</strong> after his return. Compared with his pre-injury numbers, that would mean a gain of 1.8 points, a drop of 1.0 assist and a gain of 0.4 rebounds per 36 minutes.</p>
<div class="forecast"><div class="stat"><strong>23.4</strong><span>PROJECTED POINTS / 36<br/>Before: 21.6</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>10.1</strong><span>PROJECTED ASSISTS / 36<br/>Before: 11.1</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>4.4</strong><span>PROJECTED REBOUNDS / 36<br/>Before: 4.0</span></div></div>
<p>The scoring projection surprised me. Across the 24 players, scoring tended to fall after an Achilles injury, but the model's estimate for Haliburton is actually higher than his pre-injury average. The assist estimate is slightly lower, although 10.1 assists per 36 would still be excellent playmaking production.</p>

<p>These are also <strong>per-36-minute estimates</strong>, not necessarily what he'll average each night. If he comes back on a minutes restriction, his box-score numbers could look very different.</p>
<h2>What the model cannot tell us</h2>
<p>There are some big catches. Twenty-four players is not a lot, especially when you're mixing different eras and different kinds of players. The numbers also depend on which seasons count as 'before' and 'after.' And even if a player's per-36 stats look good, that doesn't tell us whether he's moving the same way, defending as well or staying healthy enough to play a full season.</p>
<p>The model's historical errors are another reminder that individual outcomes are hard to forecast. This isn't a medical prediction about Haliburton's recovery.</p>
<p>I don't expect any model to get everything right. The point is to use the numbers to ask better questions about what happens when he returns.</p>
<h2>The Achilles heel</h2>
<p>When people talk about Achilles, they remember his heel. When people talk about a player tearing his Achilles, it's easy to let that one injury overshadow everything he did before it. I don't think that's fair to the player, and I don't think the numbers support it either.</p>
<p>Some players never got back to where they were. Others changed how they played. And a few came back looking almost impossible to slow down. That's what makes Haliburton's return worth watching.</p>
<p>I don't know if Haliburton will come back as exactly the same player. He might have to change parts of his game, and his numbers might not look the same right away. But after looking at these players, I don't think it's fair to assume his best basketball is already behind him. I'm looking forward to seeing how his comeback actually plays out.</p>
